] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415674 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "77c94cbf-f9ef-59ad-9086-7c8d91862986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415677 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=17,$ ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=7,$ and ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=5,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28b25a72-e404-5d69-993b-a9b2c8d9ba39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415677 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 17 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+5-{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 17 & =-7+5-{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 19 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-19.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415679 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4d/4d1c20f44c7a092275f200737813e11c762e1f3e80ba22ded29b41731e22472e.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of two line segments, as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81ff799c-e275-561c-accd-b7005d2a330b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415679 #edb/ref 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{1}=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=4-0=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415681 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415681 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415681 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/05/05b76357203cf2d7ef38b4bfa8daa9e0ce21b248f150e1a2d516f1a4c4e5bef3.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter circle of radius $1,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(1,1),$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415681 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4b4530c-a5d4-5bda-8a1d-c3d8365018f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415681 #edb/ref 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=1-0=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415682 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415682 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/77/77c069c4b4306b3337a44ddfc53ecd4d727e78da09d77d61e84fc01d3b30c302.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the closed path along the square of \nside $2,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caee1f70-9af3-5daf-82bf-4723f96ead82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415682 #edb/ref 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, the initial and terminal points are the same (since the path is closed and is traversed only once). Therefore, ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$ and we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186426490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186426491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186426492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186426493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186426494 #edb/ref 17592186045417]